  16 Sep 2026, VENDOR-FIRST, src/codec/rar29.rs: `Unpack29::
  decode_member_from_reader` refills the packed stream mid-member
  instead of draining the reader to EOF into one Vec first, so a
  compressed RAR 2.9-4.x member no longer costs its whole packed size in
  heap. It holds a 1 MiB window (STREAM_INPUT_WINDOW) and compact()
  drops what is consumed. The refill is PROACTIVE, not error-driven: the
  bit reader has no mid-symbol rollback, so resuming after a
  NeedMoreInput would corrupt output, which is worse than the
  allocation. The decoder instead pauses at a clean symbol boundary
  whenever fewer than STREAM_INPUT_MARGIN (128 KiB) are held and more
  input can still arrive; that margin is sized against the widest
  excursion, read_vm_code's 16-bit length field, which is read before
  MAX_VM_CODE_SIZE is checked. PPMd blocks are deliberately still
  buffered - no provable margin exists for them - and so is the member
  tail, which remaining_bits_are_zero reads whole and which leaves the
  reader at EOF where read_to_end used to. Measured on a 146 MB
  single-member -m3 set: peak RSS 288.6 MB -> 21.95 MB and flat in
  member size, wall 0.947x median / 0.976x minimum. Output byte-
  identical over 37 archive groups; eight new lib tests compare the
  CARRIED DECODER STATE as well as the bytes, because on this engine
  byte-identical output is not evidence. Ported to the fork as 47cb5a7
  on perf (1128 passed there), in a worktree off the current tip - the
  first port went onto a local perf 166 commits stale and was thrown
  away. See VENDORING.md and the host repo's
  research/RAR29-MIDSTREAM-REFILL-2026-09-16.md.
